# {{robot_title}}

{{robot_description}}

A Bluecopa RPA robot. `main.py` → SDK `launch()` → `{{RobotClass}}.run_robot()` in
`rpa/{{robot_module}}.py`.

> **Building or deploying this?** Read [`docs/building-a-bluecopa-robot.md`](docs/building-a-bluecopa-robot.md) first.

## Quick start (local)

Use Python 3.9 to match the SDK wheel.

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
.venv\Scripts\python -m pip install libs\*.whl
```

Copy the sample config/params (real ones are git-ignored) and fill them in:

```powershell
copy secrets\config.sample.json secrets\config.json
copy secrets\params.sample.json secrets\params.json
```

Run it:

```powershell
python main.py run --config secrets\config.json --params secrets\params.json --input-file-path dummy --output-folder-path .\output
```

The stub writes a placeholder file into `output\` so you can confirm the wiring before
writing real logic.

## Layout

```
{{robot_module}}/
  main.py                 # entrypoint: launch({{RobotClass}}())
  Dockerfile              # python:3.9-slim + SDK wheel
  requirements.txt
  libs/                   # the Bluecopa RPA SDK wheel
  rpa/
    {{robot_module}}.py   # {{RobotClass}} — your run_robot() logic
    spec.json             # setup-form schema
    Exceptions/
  secrets/                # *.sample.json templates (real config/params git-ignored)
  docs/
    building-a-bluecopa-robot.md
```

## Deploy

The robot ships as a Docker image pushed to an ECR repository your backend/infra team
creates for it. The registry and repo path are client-specific — see
[`docs/building-a-bluecopa-robot.md`](docs/building-a-bluecopa-robot.md) §7.
